      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© Georg Thieme Verlag KG Stuttgart · New York.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND AND STUDY AIMS",
            "content": "Routine esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD) is increasingly performed without sedation. Transoral (TO) and transnasal (TN) EGD offer different patient comfort and complications."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
            "content": "For a controlled, randomized, clinical trial comparing TN-EGD with TO-EGD without sedation, patients were assigned to TN-EGD using a thin endoscope (group 1, 93 patients), or TO-EGD using a standard endoscope (group 2, 90 patients). Physician-rated procedural time and complications as well as patient-rated side effects and preferences were compared. In group 3, patients (118) who had previously undergone TO-EGD, now underwent TN-EGD."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Between group 1 and 2 there was no significant difference for procedural time. Nausea (p\u200a=\u200a0.047) and epistaxis (p\u200a<\u200a0.001) were significantly more frequent for TN-EGD. Conversion rate from TN- to TO-EGD was low with 4.3\u200a%. For TN-EGD, patients' tolerance was better (p\u200a<\u200a0.001), gagging was less (p\u200a<\u200a0.001). In case of a future EGD, patients who know both procedures (group 3), strongly vote for TN-EGD (80\u200a%). All groups vote against sedation for future procedures (90\u200a%/90\u200a%/89\u200a%)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Epistaxis can be relevant after TN-EGD, but can mostly be managed conservatively. TN-EGD is superior to TO-EGD regarding subjective and objective gagging as well as procedural tolerance. Patients who experienced both access routes, prefer TN-EGD. TN-EGD without sedation should be aspired for patient comfort and is recommended for routine use."
          }
        ]
      },
